---
import GuidePage from '../../../components/GuidePage.astro';

const guide = {
  title: 'Uganda Travel Tips',
  slug: 'uganda-travel-tips',
  kicker: 'Essential practical advice for your visit',
  heroImg: '/images/wildlife/giraffe-uganda-murchison.jpg',
  heroImgAlt: 'Rothschild\'s giraffe on the savannah at Murchison Falls National Park Uganda',
  description: 'Essential Uganda travel tips: currency, mobile data, tipping etiquette, health advice, cultural customs, transport, and practical information for planning your Uganda safari.',
  readTime: '7 min read',
  breadcrumb: 'Uganda Travel Tips',
  intro: [
    'Uganda is a welcoming, relatively easy country to travel in, but a few practical details make a significant difference to your experience. This guide covers the most important practical information for first-time visitors to Uganda.',
  ],
};

const sections = [
  {
    heading: 'Currency and Money',
    body: [
      'The Ugandan Shilling (UGX) is the local currency. As of 2025, USD 1 is approximately UGX 3,700. The exchange rate fluctuates so check a live rate before travel.',
      'USD is the de facto currency for safaris and tourism. All lodges, park fees, gorilla permits and most tour operators quote and accept USD. Small transactions in towns and markets use Ugandan Shillings.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'Bring USD cash in small denominations (USD 1, 5, 10, 20) for tips and markets',
      'USD 50 and USD 100 notes from 2009 or newer are widely accepted; older notes may be refused',
      'ATMs in Kampala (Stanbic, DFCU, Centenary) dispense UGX reliably',
      'Limited ATM access in rural areas near national parks — withdraw enough in Kampala',
      'Visa and Mastercard accepted at most lodges and hotels',
      'Mobile money (MTN Mobile Money, Airtel Money) is widely used for local payments',
    ],
    tip: 'Carry USD 10 to 15 per person per day specifically for tipping your driver-guide and lodge staff. This is expected and constitutes a meaningful portion of their income.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Mobile Connectivity and SIM Cards',
    body: [
      'Uganda has reasonable mobile coverage in Kampala and along major highways, and limited but improving coverage in or near national parks. Deep inside parks and remote forests, there is no signal.',
      'MTN Uganda and Airtel Uganda are the two main operators. MTN generally has better coverage in national park areas. A Uganda SIM card with a data bundle is the most cost-effective solution for connectivity.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'SIM cards available at Entebbe Airport arrivals hall and in Kampala for approximately USD 1',
      'MTN is recommended — best coverage near parks',
      'Data bundles: 5GB for approximately USD 4 to 5',
      'Passport required to register a SIM card',
      'WhatsApp works well on 4G in urban areas and along major roads',
      'No signal inside most national parks — download offline maps before travel',
      'Most mid-range and luxury lodges have WiFi, though speeds vary',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Health and Medications',
    body: [
      'Uganda is a tropical country with several health considerations. Proper preparation before travel significantly reduces risk.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'Malaria is present throughout Uganda — take prophylaxis prescribed by your doctor',
      'Yellow Fever vaccination is mandatory for entry — carry your Yellow Card certificate',
      'DEET-based insect repellent (30%+ concentration) is essential — apply daily, especially at dusk',
      'Typhoid, Hepatitis A, and Tetanus vaccinations are recommended',
      'Drink only bottled or lodge-filtered water — never tap water',
      'Avoid ice in drinks at local restaurants',
      'Medical facilities are limited outside Kampala — carry adequate personal medications',
      'Comprehensive travel insurance with emergency medical evacuation cover is essential',
    ],
    tip: 'Consult a travel health clinic or your GP at least 4 to 6 weeks before departure to allow time for vaccinations and to obtain malaria prophylaxis prescriptions.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Tipping Etiquette',
    body: [
      'Tipping is standard practice in Uganda\'s tourism industry. It is not legally required but is culturally expected and forms a meaningful part of guide and lodge staff income.',
    ],
    table: [
      { col1: 'Driver-guide (full day)',       col2: 'USD 10 to 15 per person' },
      { col1: 'Lodge staff (per stay)',         col2: 'USD 5 to 10 per person' },
      { col1: 'Porter in Bwindi',              col2: 'USD 15 to 20 for the day' },
      { col1: 'Ranger guide (gorilla trek)',    col2: 'USD 10 to 20 per person' },
      { col1: 'Restaurant (sit-down meal)',     col2: '10% of bill if no service charge' },
