  },
  {
    heading: 'Transport in Uganda',
    body: [
      'Uganda\'s road network has improved significantly in recent years but many roads to national parks remain unpaved, especially in the wet season. Private 4x4 transport is the standard for safaris.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'All L\'okawa safari vehicles are private 4x4 Land Cruisers with roof hatches',
      'Kampala to Bwindi: approximately 8 hours by road',
      'Kampala to Murchison Falls: approximately 5 hours by road',
      'Kampala to Kibale Forest: approximately 5 hours by road',
      'Boda bodas (motorcycle taxis) are available in towns but not recommended for tourists unfamiliar with local road conditions',
      'Matatu (shared minibus): used by locals, not practical for safari visitors',
      'Charter flights available to Kidepo, Pakuba (Murchison), and Kisoro (near Bwindi)',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Cultural Customs and Etiquette',
    body: [
      'Ugandans are generally warm, welcoming and patient with visitors. A few cultural customs are worth knowing.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'Greet people before asking questions or making requests — hello and thank you in Luganda are "Oli Otya" and "Webale"',
      'Dress modestly outside safari lodges and national parks, especially in rural areas and Kampala',
      'Always ask permission before photographing local people — many will say yes happily',
      'Remove shoes when entering private homes and some community spaces',
      'Public displays of affection are generally frowned upon outside tourist areas',
      'Bargaining is acceptable in markets but not in lodges, restaurants or with professional guides',
      'The left hand is considered impolite for giving or receiving items — use the right hand',
    ],
    tip: 'Learning even a few words of Luganda or the local language in the area you are visiting (Rukiga near Bwindi, Rutooro near Kibale) will be genuinely appreciated and often generates warm smiles.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Electricity and Practical Details',
    body: ['Quick reference for practical planning.'],
    bullets: [
      'Power sockets: British 3-pin Type G at 240V — bring a universal adapter',
      'Power cuts are common in Uganda — lodges typically have backup generators',
      'Most lodges have USB charging points; bring a multi-port USB charger',
      'Time zone: East Africa Time (UTC+3) — no daylight saving',
      'Driving is on the left side of the road',
      'Emergency number: 999 (police), 112 (general emergency)',
      'Uganda Wildlife Authority: +256 414 355 000',
    ],
  },
